.TH FACTOTUM 4
.SH NAME
factotum \- the keys a session holds, and the handshakes run through them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B factotum
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Factotum
posts
.B /srv/factotum
and serves two files, mounted at
.BR /mnt/factotum .
A key is a line written to
.BR ctl :
.IP
.EX
key proto=noise user=glenda dom=home !passphrase=...
key proto=noise user=one dom=home !private=<64 hex digits>
.EE
.PP
The first derives the private key from the passphrase with argon2id,
salted with
.IR user @ dom ,
and forgets the passphrase; the second takes the key as given, which is
how
.IR init (8)
loads a host's key from
.BR /adm/hostkey .
A read of
.B ctl
lists every key's public half and no secret.
.PP
A conversation on
.B rpc
runs one Noise IK handshake a line at a time, hex for every byte string,
so the program running it holds no key:
.IP
.EX
start initiator user=U dom=D remote=<hex>   ->  msg <hex>
msg <hex>                                   ->  done <peer> <send> <recv>
start responder user=U dom=D                ->  ok
msg <hex>                                   ->  msg <hex>
finish                                      ->  done <peer> <send> <recv>
.EE
.PP
.B done
names the far side by its static public key and hands over the two
transport keys, the initiator's sending key first, which
.B sys/libauth
seals the stream with.
A write that cannot be honoured fails with
.B EINVAL
and the next read says why.
The ephemeral key each handshake needs comes from
.BR /dev/random .
.SH SOURCE
.B servers/factotum/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR srv (4),
.IR import (4),
.IR exportfs (4)
.SH BUGS
One factotum per machine, started by
.IR init ,
holds the host's key; a person's passphrase goes into the same one.
Per-session factotums arrive with logins.
